Dear Anna 
&nbsp;
Thank you for forwarding the message from the leads.&nbsp; It makes very dispiriting reading, and suggests that we are in a very difficult position.&nbsp;

&nbsp;
The first point we must stress is that neither of us are employment lawyers, and the issues the leads are raising clearly involve issues of employment law on which it reads as if they have probably taken advice.&nbsp; We cannot advise you on
 such issues, as they are outside our expertise. 
&nbsp;
We would in any event be in a position of conflict, in that we have pre-existing relationships with some of the leads, and certainly know two them well having worked with them very closely.&nbsp;

&nbsp;
We have to say that, having read the leads’ email, it does not appear to us that there is any real prospect of them agreeing to anything.&nbsp; They appear to be raising every possible objection.&nbsp; It does not seem to us to be realistic to imagine
 that small tweaks to the drafting will get matters over the line, still less do so on the necessary timescale.&nbsp; Obviously, we are in no position to second guess what the leads are seeking to achieve, but we can see that some might suppose that their position
 is intended either to encourage you to release them from their notice periods, or else to set the stage for an argument that they have been constructively dismissed.&nbsp;

&nbsp;
In these circumstances, it seems to us that the only real options are:
&nbsp;

either to call their bluff, and for you simply to proceed to agree terms with Leigh Day – as it seems clear that the firms themselves are very close, and that the court was at least
 not misled when told that yesterday; or
&nbsp;

we have to tell the court that the agency agreement is not as close to conclusion as we had hoped, and that we overstated the position in our submissions yesterday.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
It will be appreciated that the latter will cause the defendants to scent blood.&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;
&nbsp;
Obviously, given the caveat we have expressed above about our lack of employment expertise, we cannot comment on what implications you subscribing to an agency agreement in terms that the leads object to might have, eg to any attempt on
 their part to suggest constructive dismissal.&nbsp; However, in terms of your application and for keeping the show on the road, at present it seems the less worse option.&nbsp;

&nbsp;
What is also clear is, of course, that the timetable is very constrained.&nbsp; It cannot be long before court and defendants alike start to press for the note, and as we advised you earlier we cannot file the note without correcting any error
 that has emerged in the information we gave the court yesterday.&nbsp; At the moment, it is clear that we would have to withdraw the assurance that an agency agreement was close to execution, and on the contrary would need to say to the court that it is not.&nbsp; If
 the court asks the timescale, we would have to say that we cannot say.&nbsp; That would surely lead to the defendants again contending that there is a risk the trial will collapse, given that the implication of Day 13 and Alinia 1 is that the continued involvement
 of the leads is essential to the proposed arrangements.
&nbsp;
Kind regards
