Dear Anna,&nbsp;


Thank you for your email.&nbsp;


We have some overarching points to raise before addressing the points in your email regarding the Side Letter Agency Agreement
 (“SL AA”).&nbsp;


First and foremost, we have raised serious and significant issues regarding how traumatic we, and the entire team, are finding
 this process and that we have genuine concerns for the mental health and well-being of our staff. We further flagged that the proposal creates a two-tier structure amongst the wider team, which risks alienating individual members of the staff. Your email worryingly
 does not address any of those concerns, nor does it even mention them.&nbsp;


We also note that various junior staff within the NOx department have been enlisted to send out correspondence, including on weekends,
 relating to non-common work and non-PDD Trial matters (see, for example, an email from Hannah Varghese at 15.32 on Saturday, 4 October 2025). Requiring the most junior staff to become actively involved in non-common work issues, while on the other hand attempting
 to assert that “everything we have done or are intending to do is with the best interests of our clients as well as our staff in mind first and foremost” is simply unreal. Correspondence of that nature and related to non-common work should be sent by
 an individual not working on critical workstreams.&nbsp;&nbsp;


Each of the case leads are committed to finding a co-operative solution to the issues that are being raised. That is why we are
 proactively engaging in discussions and, in aid of assisting the firm, why we have continued to raise points so as to avoid the firm falling foul of issues that they may otherwise be presented with, e.g. noting potential additional disclosure obligations re.
 documents referred to in witness statements. You also have said that you “welcome any specific input we wish to contribute”. This is difficult to contemplate as being a genuine offer in circumstances where a) the SL AA was provided by Leigh Day (“LD”),
 not you nor the Board, despite Pogust Goodhead (“PG”) being our employer; and b) where we have previously sought to provide input (e.g. meeting with Alicia Alinia at 18:00 Monday 29 September 2025) and you have not engaged with it. Instead, you have
 sent us an email that dismisses our concerns and informs us that our description of the SL AA is incorrect, while at the same time stating that your intention is for this email to now bring these discussions to a conclusion. This is neither helpful nor is
 it in the spirit of co-operation.&nbsp;&nbsp;


For the avoidance of any doubt, we do not consent to the SL AA in its current form. No representations can be made to the Court
 at the PTR on 6 October that this has been put in place and is agreed. To the extent that you and the Board believe you can bind individuals named in the SL AA (or annexes) by virtue of it being a ‘firm decision’ we disagree for reasons set out below. Every
 individual subject to the agreement either does not consent to it (the case leads) or, as far as we are aware, has never seen any version of it and so is unable to have provided consent.&nbsp; We accept of course, that Counsel can inform the Court (on 6 October
 or otherwise) that it is being drafted and the intention is that it will be put in place in due course.&nbsp;&nbsp;


If we, as the collective case leads, are to work collaboratively with the Board and you, and HR, to establish how we can move forward
 amicably, then it requires serious engagement from you and the Board, and not a dismissal of our concerns.&nbsp;&nbsp;


