Hi Jemma,&nbsp;Thanks for reviewing the other email for me earlier today – much appreciated. Please see the email chain below. Are you able to review the draft reply below too please?&nbsp;Hi Michael,&nbsp;Thank you for your emails and for outlining your concerns regarding Occupational Sick Pay (OSP). I can confirm the firm’s position regarding Occupational Sick Pay (OSP) as follows:&nbsp;Your contract makes it clear that any entitlement to pay during absence is subject to the firm’s sickness policy. That policy is not contractual, and we are not obliged to individually notify employees of its content. Employees are expected to review policies, which are made available from time to time on HiBob. The applicable policy, in place prior to your resignation and your current period of sick leave, governs any entitlement to OSP and SSP.The sickness policy uploaded on 17 November 2025 at 09:59:39 was the first formal iteration of a written sickness policy. No previous written sickness policy was in place.Condition 7(5) relating to OSP was included in this first iteration. The policy was published to clarify eligibility for occupational sick pay going forward.The firm confirms that no employee currently working their notice is in receipt of OSP under the terms of the policy.&nbsp;In line with this, the firm maintains that you are not eligible for OSP during your notice period and any entitlement is limited to Statutory Sick Pay (SSP).&nbsp;Kind regards,Natasha &nbsp;&nbsp;From: Michael Burke &lt;burke7y@hotmail.com&gt; Sent: 10 December 2025 15:03To: Natasha Geraldo Baird &lt;ngeraldobaird@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Re: M Burke&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Hi Natasha,&nbsp;I am sorry to chase again, but time is fast running out if I am to return to work, against medical advice, in order to receive full pay. The lack of response is potentially prejudicing my ability to mitigate my loss.&nbsp;&nbsp;If you can also please clarify the company's position on my unilateral contract change that would be helpful.&nbsp;Similarly, can you confirm the position re the courier who is due to arrive on Friday?&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for AndroidFrom: Michael Burke &lt;burke7y@hotmail.com&gt;Sent: Tuesday, December 9, 2025 5:43:13 PMTo: Natasha Geraldo Baird &lt;ngeraldobaird@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Re: M Burke &nbsp;Hi Natasha,&nbsp;Could you come back to me on the below at your earliest opportunity or let me know when you will be able to respond?&nbsp;&nbsp;As advised before, this is quite pressing.&nbsp;Given that we are now on day 7 of my absence (meaning the firm's position as I understand it is that I am only entitled to SSP for those 7 days as opposed to OSP), if the firm maintain that position then I will be forced to consider what options are open to me to dispute those 7 days, and any further that I assert should be paid at OSP and not SSP.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for AndroidFrom: Michael Burke &lt;burke7y@hotmail.com&gt;Sent: Monday, December 8, 2025 7:13:08 PMTo: Natasha Geraldo Baird &lt;ngeraldobaird@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Re: M Burke &nbsp;Hi Natasha,&nbsp;Thank you for this and for that information.&nbsp;Bearing all of this in mind i.e. that the policy has been changed without notification, that the policy constitutes (as you have termed it) part of my contractual framework, and that the policy makes a material change to my contractual position, is it still the firm's position that I am not eligible for OSP?&nbsp;&nbsp;I note that the firm, as is any employer, is obliged to notify their employees of any change in their contractual position.&nbsp;Kind regards,Michael&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for AndroidFrom: Natasha Geraldo Baird &lt;ngeraldobaird@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent: Monday, December 8, 2025 5:57:34 PMTo: M