Sent: 04 November 2025 16:51
To: James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;
Cc: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Dilan Alma &lt;dalma@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;
Subject: RE: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation


&nbsp;
Hi James,

&nbsp;
Thank you for your email. This is encouraging news, and we appreciate your efforts in moving this forward.
&nbsp;
With regards to the additional points, I have attached a further advice on Mercedes which may be helpful. We can also arrange for Anna and a couple of the case leads to meet with
 Toremis tomorrow to discuss further. The team have availability between 13.30 and 17.00. &nbsp;Please let me know whether Toremis are able to accommodate.
&nbsp;
With regards to the cross-collateralisation point, we do have a few questions which we are hoping you can address:
&nbsp;


Client proceeds – please could you clarify whether cross-collateralisation has any impact on client damages? Specifically, can you confirm that it does not affect client recoveries beyond the insurer’s entitlements?
Scope - how exactly will cross-collateralisation operate i.e., what types of insurer recoveries (e.g., paid claims, contingent premiums) are in scope?
Existing arrangements - would there be any impact on the wrapper arrangements already in place? Can we keep them completely separate?
We would also like to explore the
Mosaic option you mentioned yesterday in tandem. Please could you provide some additional detail on the following points:


1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Structure - the policy would need to safeguard client interests, could the policy expressly designate claimants as third-party beneficiaries? If not, would it be possible for the insurer
 to issue a side letter, deed of benefit, or assignment giving clients direct enforceable rights? &nbsp;

2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Claims - it would be helpful if you could outline the claims process, including how coverage applies when the insured is not the named party to the adverse costs order.

3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Protections for PG – we would need to ensure that the firm is not liable if the insurer declines cover for reasons unrelated to misconduct. How can PG navigate this risk?

4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Claim payments - this structure could be scrutinised by the defendants and therefore it would assist if any claims monies could be directly paid to the defendant or PGs client account.&nbsp;
